  "strategy": "We take an entirely new approach by treating the problem as a non-convex multi-modal optimization task. Instead of focusing on basis functions or normalization, we explore a diverse family of asymmetric piecewise exponential profiles built from elementary damped oscillations. The key contributions are:\n\n1. Creating sequences from phase-shifted, amplitude-modulated exponential units (EMUs)\n2. Optimizing a small set of parent exponentials by gradient descent\n3. Each sequence is the weighted sum of these EMUs (equivalent to a piecewise exponential)\n4. Accuracy enforcement via periodic validation of top candidates at varied lengths\n\nImplementation details:\n\nA. Initialization\n  - Convert existing `height_sequence_1` (if available) to array\n  - Compute reference score using exact convolution\n  - Use regularized coordinate ascent to find good initial candidates\n  - Diversify by taking three shifts of the parent sequence and three random N candidates\n\nB.Profile Construction\n  - Use unit-length exponential components (exponential with convolution maximum >1)\n  - Each component is a damped exponential with periodic boundary modifications\n  - Components are: f_m(t) = exp(-(t-m)^2/scale) for scale chosen to ensure sum>=0.01\n\nC.Optimization Procedure\n  - Choose three initial component vectors uniformly distributed\n  - For each candidate point in [0, budget_s/2]:\n        If time exhausted: break\n        Score validation of top candidate (exact)\n        Sample three new candidates (one per component) and optimize mantissas\n  - Use stochastic gradient descent with adaptive learning rate schedules on normalized vectors\n\nD.Gradient Computation\n  - For a given normalized segment, compute analytic gradient of 2*n*max(conv(segment))/sum(segment)^2\n  - Use automatic differentiation via softmax formulation in gradient space\n  - Each candidate length n is handled separately\n\nE.Search Choices\n  - Initialize with both the parent sequence and three new component seeds\n  - Sample from 8 different starting points (three from parent, five from random)\n  - Use one-step lookahead before stepping through budget\n\nF.Validation Strategy\n  - For each interior point, perform a full evaluation only if cost improves\n  - Save time by validating only the best candidate from the end of each phase\n  - Maintain best solution from all trials\n\nG.Cost Function Regulation\n  - We redefine the cost to take into account sequence length only for exact validation\n  - During optimization, we work on normalized components (maintaining scale)\n  - After optimization, we normalize the sequence to ensure sum>=0.01\n\nH.Budget Allocation\n  - For mixed-length searches, allocate two-thirds budget for optimization\n  - Use restarts when optimization plateaus\n\nI.Fallback Mechanism\n  - Using a standard cubic-spline optimized at scale 5 (three knots)\n  - Compute fallback and validate exactly\n\nJustification Summary:\nThis approach might outperform the parent by using dynamic feature scaling and analytic exponential gradients. It explores higher-dimensional optimization spaces via elementary components.\n\nExecution Pseudocode:\n\n```\nimport numpy as np\nimport time\n\ndef propose_candidate(seed=42, budget_s=1800):\n    if 'height_sequence_1' in globals():\n        parent = np.array(height_sequence_1)\n    else:\n        parent = None\n\n    t0 = time.time()\n    timeout = t0 + budget_s\n\n    # Evaluate parent\n    if parent.size > 0:\n        [points], score = evaluate_sequence(parent)\n        if np.isfinite(score) and score < 0:\n            best_candidate = parent\n            best_score = score\n\n    # Generate unit gains (EMUs)\n    if best_candidate is None:\n        # Three unit arrival curves\n        best_candidates = []\n        for n in [4, 16, 128]:\n            # Create simplex candidates\n            pass\n\n    # Select distinct starting points (5 in total)\n    subspace = best_candidate * sample_vector\n    subdir = subspace[:3]\n    boundaries = [min, max]\n\n    # Optimization loop\n    while time.time() < timeout:\n        # Do one full cycle of three EMUs and three admmatters regions\n\n        return best_candidate, best_score\n```\n\nKey Differentiators:\n1. We use a fundamentally different sequence generation approach\n2. Focus on elementary exponential units with damped oscillations\n3. Less reliance on normalization/surrogates (more expectations)\n4. Better time distribution across length scales",
